d shown  works well and so for the the procedure  that the pathologist had worked out  was to cut and manually stainless slides  then to view those slides at high  magnification  on their microscope and then they would  identify the regions of high vascularity  in the tumor tissue  acquire and save 10 high power fields of  view from their  microscope and then score the images in  photoshop  and altogether each case took the  pathologists about an hour of time  we wanted to dramatically cut this down  so the automated procedure that we came  up with  replaced the manual staining with  automated staining on an autom on a  high-throughput  autostainer and then we replaced the  viewing and acquiring of  images on a microscope with a digital  whole slide scan  we then used software in order to  identify and segment  the invasive tumor from the stroma and  to identify the macrophages and blood  vessels within the tumor  and then finally we identified the tmm  structures based on their morphology  and their juxtapositioning and then  finally we would  cut that tissue up into the equivalence  of  these high high power fields of view and  rank and score the tmm  in the top 10 fields top 10 ranking  fields  all in all we were able to reduce the  amount of pathologist time from about an  hour per case  to down to about five minutes mostly  just for quality control  and so in order to understand this whole  process i'll walk you through it next  but in order to understand the process  let me just mention briefly what digital  pathology is and how we're using it  so digital pathology uses digital whole  slide scanning  it's basically a machine that can go and  scan the entire tissue  by acquiring thousands of uh high  resolution  images that span the entire tissue and  then stitch them together to create  one giant super image it then creates  down sampled versions of those images  so that you can seamlessly switch  between  magnifications very much like you're  using google ear