th where you can  view the world and then a country and  then  somebody an individual house and so  the real power of digital pathology is  not just though in viewing the tissue  it's that we can actually perform single  cell  quantitative analysis on any one of  these individual tiles  and then apply that analysis tissue wide  and one of the software packages that we  use  to do this is viz by by vizio farm  so what does the process actually look  like well  first we start with the digital whole  slide scan  so here's the entire microscope slide  we can then zoom in and zoom in and zoom  in again until you get down to the  single cell level  we did this on our 3d his tech panoramic  p250  and we scanned at uh 40x taking  6500 fields in really under three  minutes  um then we developed a series of custom  developed apps within uh viz from visio  farm  and the first one was just to perform  the invasive tumor identification and  segmentation  so we know where we want to apply our  future analyses  and then we analyzed that tissue at very  high magnification  using color decompolation to identify  the stain  of all of the the tissue using k-means  clustering we could classify  each and the the color in each and every  pixel  into one of the categories of blood  vessels macrophages  tumor or stroma and at that point  we could use the morphometric analysis  capabilities of physiopharm  in order to identify the macrophages and  blood vessels  that were in direct contact with each  other and in direct contact with tumor  cells around  and here's just a zoom in of one of  those where you can see on the left  the identification of the tumor cells  the the macrophages and the endothelial  cells  and on the right what visio form was  able to identify in an automated fashion  and then we apply that single cell  analysis across the entire tissue  at that point we can cut up the tissue  and rank and  score and rank all of the fields of view  and then  the algorithm is able to present us with  the 10  top ranked fields of view from which we  can generate an overall skull  score for the whole tissue so now that  we have an algorithm and we have a  a process a method for evaluating tmm we  wanted to  validate that process how well does it  perform how does it compare  to what the pathologists were doing  manually  so we again turn uh we're now using this  digital pathology for analytical  validation  and so in order to do this what we did  was we picked out several measures of  reproducibility and accuracy  and evaluated them in order to see how  each step of that process was being  performed  and of course you know the first thing  that we wanted to do was to evaluate how  well do  pathologists perform the t-mem  identification  and so what we did was we took 100 test  fields of view that contained a variety  of numbers of tmem doorways in them and  we gave them to two pathologists on two  different days and we asked them to to  score them  and what we found is that within  observer right so one pathologist on day  one and day two  they agree with themselves uh to a high  degree  with 96 of the time uh on for both  pathologists they agree with themselves  on the next day  but they also agree between themselves  so there's a high level of agreement 92  percent of the time  between pathologist 1 and pathologist 2.  next we wanted to ask how well can the  algorithm do a similar kind of thing so  we gave  all of the slides over to the algorithm  scanned them  twice on two separate days and we asked  how reproducibly  could the algorithm identify those team  m doorways  and what we find is unsurprisingly  because it's a machine  the day-to-day reproducibility was  perfect agreeing with itself 100 of the  time  the machine does what the machine does  right so  the next step was to ask how well  does the algorithm perform in  identifying  tmem structures within a field of view  that's given to it so you  give the algorithm one particular image  and you say  count up the number of of tmm doorways  in this  how well does it prepare how well does  it perform doing that  so we gave we picked out 10 fields of  view  per case that were selected by one  pathologist  and we gave them to the machine and to a  pathologist another pathologist for team  mem scoring  and what we found is that we also get a  high level  of correlation between the scores that  are generated  about 86 percent of the time they agree  finally we wanted to test the whole  procedure and the whole procedure  involves  giving a pathologist the entire  microscope slide and saying  there's no restrictions go look anywhere  you want on the slide  and go pick out a field of view score it  for the tm square and return me  a result for the entire slide and then  we do the same thing  for for the machine algorithm and so we  did this  we actually did this with five different  pathologists scoring and then we  compared the average of those five  scores to  the the machine generated algorithm and  what we found is actually an even higher  level of  correlation between the pathologists and  the algorithm  where they agree about 94 of the time  and  we believe that this is higher than the  86 because  we use the average of five different  pathologist scores  and it seems like the the pathologist to  pathologist  variation seem to be driving down the  score when we we just compared  individual fields of view  okay so now that we have this validated  test it's ready to go into the clinic  we decided we're going to go and see  what what would be the next thing  that we could do to move this uh  to improve upon the performance of these  biomarkers  and so this is our current work that  we're probably in the next week or so  going to submit for publication  um it's been the work of dr shan jun yeh  who's a postdoc a very talented postdoc  in the lab and um he was asking the  question  can we actually improve that  prognostication how would we go about  doing that  and so the way to do it the the  rationale  is you can get to the rationale by  thinking about  what these biomarkers are actually  telling us biologically  so the tmem biomarker is a  quantification  of the number of doorways into the  vasculature that there  are inside of a primary tumor and the  mean of calc  is a quantification or measure of the  level of cell motility  for the tumor cells that are in there  but what you realize  through this then is that if  uh if you have no motile cells but you  have lots of  team m doorways you're not going to get  a lot of metastasis because none  there are no moving cells to pass  through those doorways  and and go to secondary sites similarly  if you have um no tmm doorways but lots  of moving cells  those cells can't go anywhere they can't  get access to the the vasculature  and so in this case no team in doorways  means that you're not going to have an  appreciable level of metastasis  only when you have both  motile cells and an appreciable number  of tmm doorways so that those cells can  get into the vasculature  will you get hematogenous spread and  metastasis  and so we asked the question then how  can we  effectively take these two  mechanistically linked biomarkers and  combine them  in order to to take advantage of this  insight so a simple way of course to do  this  is to say we're going to combine or call  this combined marker high  when both of the individual tests are  high  and high is really determined by a cut  off threshold  that effectively separates a patient  population  between a group that where they're not  going to have metastatic recurrence and  a group where they are going to  and so for each individual score for  each individual biomarker  we can say that a tmem score for a  particular case  is considered high if it's above that  cutoff and low  if it's below that cut off and similar  for  meena calc the combined marker then  would be high if both tmem  and minicalc for that particular patient  is high  and low in all other cases  but before we can just go ahead with  that simple uh method  there's another complication which is  that t-mem and meaning calc  are measured uh differently  tmem is actually measured  only in the top ten fields whereas mean  a calc  is measured across the entire slide  and so there could be several different  ways of combining team mem  and mean a calc they may there may be  spatial associations you may need to  have  um team amino calc measured in the exact  same region  or there may be no spatial connection as  at all in addition we don't know what  the  impact of tissue heterogeneity is how  much of that tissue  do you need to acquire is ten fields of  view enough or do you need to go larger  and finally we want so so given all this  we wanted to test a number of different  ways  of combining all of these markers  and so what we did was we started out by  defining  what are the different tests that we're  actually going to to try out and  evaluate  so we started by defining the extent of  the tissue which is really a way of  accounting for tissue heterogeneity we  can either measure  the entire tissue or we could have our  pathologist  limit that area to where they felt that  would be  most representative of that tissue  and then within each of those regions of  interest  we could either analyze the number of  tmm doorways across the entire  region of interest or we could just pick  out the top 10 fields of view  in each one so all in all if you number  these one two three and four  it gave us four different tests that we  could evaluate  and compare and once we evaluated those  of course  now we have to mix in the mean of calc  in those same areas and but there's an  additional consideration  which is that prior studies using nina  calc  limited the quantification of the  signals to just the cytoplasm  by a cytokeratin mask and it's really  never been tested whether this was  necessary so we additionally wanted to  to test that out  and so given those four different areas  and the two different possibilities for  the  cytokeratin mask that leaves eight  different combinations for mean italic  so we have four tmm analyses and eight  meaning calc analyses  that results in 32 different  combinations  that's a lot of combinations so what we  said though is  that not all 32 combinations are really  consistent  in roi type and tissue coverage so we  wanted to only test those things  that used the same uh roi type and  tissue coverage for both  uh both individual biomarkers  and so that paired the 32 choices down  to only eight  which used the same roi type and tissue  coverage so how do we evaluate which one  is best  well the the first step is to evaluate  how  each uh individual biomarker tmm and  meaning calc  separate the patient population based  upon  time to distance recurrence and so how  do we do this what we do is we adjust  that cut point  which which defines uh whether a patient  is in the high  group or in the low group and then we  look at how they fared over time  using a kaplan-meier analysis and what  we see  when we do this is that the t-mem and  nina calc  tests alone they work fairly well they  create  we can select cut points for all of them  and get the p values  and then we can combine them using that  uh text of the logical end  operation that i mentioned before to get  the p values for the combined markers  but it's a little bit difficult to just  tell whether there's any improvement  in um in by by just looking at the  p-values  and so what we did was we developed a  better metric which is to convert those  p-values  to fold changes and the way we do that  is we take the ratio of tmem to the  combined marker  or the mean account to the combined  marker analysis to generate  fold changes in performance gain right  and what you can see from this is that  the combined marker  almost always improves prognostication  versus tmem  or the meaning calc analysis alone  and you can see that because all of the  numbers are larger than one except for  the team  m4 mc8 which is a 0.5 so only in that  one case does it not outperform the  individual  biomarker and what you can also see is  that  you actually get a better improvement in  general  for meena calc than you do for tmem the  numbers are are typically higher for  uh fold improvements for the performance  gain  in mean account than for tina  so which one is actually best well in  order to narrow it down  we decided to highlight um just those  scores  where team m the team gain is greater  than 5  and the mean a calculate in is greater  than 10.  and this yields two winners we can see  the team m1  mc2 is a winner and team m4 mc7  is also a winner but if you look at the  values team m4 mc7 has a fold gain of  11.3  and 6.9 for the two over the two  individual biomarkers  whereas the other test the team m1 mc2  has a performance gain of 175 and 187  so the best clear the clear best  performer gives us a greater than  hundred and seventy fold improvement  over both team and nina cal colon  so how well does that best performer  separate the population  um well you can see here that the tmem  and the meaning calc analysis are able  to separate the populations  uh somewhat well but when we evaluate  the same cohort with the combined marker  analysis  we can see a dramatic improvement in in  segregation between those two  separation between those two groups so  the combined marker analysis  dramatically improves the prognosis of  distant metastases  and what does that winner look like will  the best performer analyze tmem over the  entire tissue  and excluded the nuclear menical signals  by using  the cytokeratin mask and this really  indicates to us that the tissue  heterogeneity  and non-specific meanest staining are  crucial factors  so in summary i told you about how we  discovered these new biomarkers from  metastasis  and how we did clinical validation of  these biomarkers  using digital pathology to show  reproducibility and to analytically  validate them  and then how now we're trying to use  digital pathology to improve  prognostication  and all of this work has really been  made possible by the gruff liquor  biophotonic center  and its associated integrated imaging  program at einstein  where the mandates are to develop novel  technologies and to use those novel  technologies in order to bridge to the  clinic  and what i've told you today is just one  story that we have in collaboration with  the department of pathology  but there are several others with  department of surgery and  our healthcare industry partners and  collaborators both  across the u.s and abroad i'll end up  with just showing you the clinical  and basic science research team which  spans a number of different institutions  that work together under the auspices of  the new york pathology oncology group  and i'll finish up with just displaying  my acknowledgment slide  and take any questions that you guys  have thank you  and thank you david for that informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  and joining david is regan baird the  regional manager for  vizio farm east i want to remind our  audience members that if you have any  questions you want to ask  please do so now just click on that ask  a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for  so let's take a look reading david we  have quite a few questions  already coming in our first question  is the two tests that you've developed  use different modalities tmem  is an immunohistochemical  stain and minicalc is an amino  fluorescent  stain why not make them ihc  or bose i s  great thanks thanks um for the question  so um  yes they're both taken in different  modalities  but they are fundamentally measuring  different things about the tissue so  um immunohistochemical stains are  typically not very quantitative  in the stain the the process of the  development of the chromogen  actually is a pretty non-linear process  whereas immunofluorescence  the amount of light signal that you get  from a  a chroma from a fluorophore can be  quantified and related directly to  the protein expression level and so uh  for meeting a calc where we're really  interested in the level of the isoform  expression we use the immunofluorescence  however for the t m stain what we're  really more interested in  is the morphological uh um  the morphology of the tissue  that is the juxtaposition of the three  different cell types so we're really  looking in that case more for a yes or a  no  answer to is this particular cell a  macrophage or is it a tumor cell and  what is it next to  and so that's why uh we really use these  different um  the two different modalities  [Music]  as part of our uh second study where we  compared  uh the two biomarkers we used and tried  to create the combined marker  uh it was really important since we were  using those two different  modalities to be able to use some of the  to align features within visio farm that  really could align those two different  images  the two slides down to the single cell  level and that gave us the ability to  have confidence  that we were looking at the same region  sometimes the same cell  within the tissue in order to be able to  evaluate  both the morphology of of the team  and the expression level of the isoform  of protein isoforms  thank you so much now what is the  hypothesis  for the clinical value of the tmm  results for guiding  the decisions of management of patient  therapy  well thanks that's actually another good  question sometimes it's not so  clear you know why you would want to be  able to specifically measure  the level of dissemination and really  the answer is because  metastasis and metastatic spread  is really what is responsible for  the overwhelming majority of mortality  in  breast cancer the problem  that clinicians face is that they don't  know  who is going to have metastatic disease  and who isn't and so  pretty much everyone who uh  has tumors of a certain characteristics  or over a certain size  or a certain grade winds up getting  chemotherapy  just because we don't know who  who has metastatic disease and who  doesn't and so that  leads to potentially to a lot of over  treatment of patients and and these  treatments can be  very costly in terms of money  they're expensive treatments but also in  terms of quality of life  they're very harsh they are you know  cytotoxic  therapies um and so if we can reduce the  amount of  over treatment then that will have a  very significant impact upon  lives of patients in addition there's  also a smaller subset  of patients who potentially would have  metastatic disease  but it's not apparent from their primary  tumor and so  the tests like meena calc and tmem are  really designed  to to try to identify that particular  population  that's going to have uh disseminating  disease and  would benefit the most from uh  from cytotoxic chemotherapies and also  you know to  to identify those that would not benefit  thank you very much and i want to also  thank our audience members for these  great questions  coming in our next question from an  audience member  is there a difference in the mental  health value  obtained from different parts of tumors  or  different areas of the tumor that may be  hypoxic  so i guess the question is really about  the heterogeneity of  of the munich calc and  part of what we tried to address in this  second study with the combined marker is  what really what is the impact of tissue  heterogeneity  on the the prognostic ability of this  biomarker and  what we're finding is that um tissue  heterogeneity exists  and uh it is actually a bit detrimental  to  our our ability to prognosticate and so  the  solution that we've um believe we've  struck upon  with the results of our combined marker  analysis is that  the um is that  uh taking the mean account  or averaging or the mean a calc over the  entire tissue slide  is probably the best way to go and that  way you you tend to  um average out the areas that are  um a heterogeneous and  areas like hypoxic areas we're finding  in other studies  actually do have an impact upon  both tmm formation and meaning calc  expression  and so that these are these conditions  of hypoxia  wind up leading to increased levels of  dissemination  thank you very much now how does your  combined  biomarker analysis relate to  local relapse and lymph node metastasis  compared to distant metastasis  so what we have been studying  is uh really um  geared toward an analysis of distant  metastasis  um local relapse is best measured  by other metrics of that are more  closely related to  growth so for example i showed the  comparison to oncotype dx this is a  a gene analysis which  really tell us about the growth  potential what are the  cell proliferation genes that are up  regulated inside of the tumor  and that would wind up leading to local  relapse  the difference between lymph node  metastasis and distant metastases  it's not clear but an additional  complication  to that is that it's been recently  published by  our group and by other groups that  dissemination hematogenous dissemination  can also occur from secondary sites like  lymph node metastases  and in particular in a study that we  published just last year  what we discovered was that um tm  doorways actually exist  can be visualized inside of uh  of lymph node metastases and so um  the presence of tumor metastases inside  of lymph nodes  could be an indicator of  a further spread and  that's due to team mem and so  by being able to  assess the level of team m doorway and  mean account involvement  in the primary tumor it could  potentially inform you  about uh the dissemination  um uh competence of of um  lymph node mets as well  thank you so much and we have time for  one more question  a